* From node.js [+documentation+|https://nodejs.org/api/events.html#eventtarget-error-handling]:

 “When an error occurs within an EventEmitter instance, the typical action is for an 'error' event to be emitted. These are treated as special cases within [+Node.js+|http://node.js].

If an EventEmitter does not have at least one listener registered for the 'error' event, and an 'error' event is emitted, the error is thrown, a stack trace is printed, and the Node.js process exit.”

* So what I think happens is:
** client.end() triggers the underlying socket for ssh to disconnect
** In specific network conditions sock.on(‘end’ ..) and sock.on(‘close’ ..) that will emit the “Connection lost before handshake” error since onDone is called ([+link+|https://github.com/mscdex/ssh2/blob/dd5510c0888476e9eec205273732473fcce5adc6/lib/client.js#L753C24-L753C56])
** In SftpClient we are removing all listeners (including the error one) and resolving the promise right after calling end() on the client ([+link+|https://github.com/bolteu/bolt-server/blob/06026b390e6e90913a2ce08bbd3cf72d8ca56e49/src/delivery/provider/delivery_provider_portal/main/api/SftpClient.ts#L49])
** Since the error event is emitted when the socket asynchronous disconnects and the error listener is already removed, [+node.js+|http://node.js] will throw an error and crash the process (expected per docs)
* Not sure how to fix this, but some solutions might be:
** a) Creating a new Client for ssh every time (when connect() is called) and let the GC remove the old client instance + listeners
** b) Remove the listeners and resolve the promise in SftpClient.end() only after the socket closes. Something like:

{noformat}this.client.once("close", () => {
     this.client.removeAllListeners();
     resolve();
});{noformat}

(not sure if close is called every time though)

