Making a Power Profile is not a viable option since the minimum current to ride up a hill is 14A, and most of the batteries shut off even below that.

We must consider raising the minimum voltage of the SOC calculation to 34V. Since the IoT can’t read what battery the scooter is using, there is no way of knowing if the battery is old or not. On some of the batteries, we might lose around 30% SOC, but at the same time, the problem of SOC dropping to 0% will be solved.



Next steps:

* Improve SOC calculation for better battery compatibility.
* Define the minimum voltage to be 34V.
* Determine the capacity at a 0.2C load until 34V by testing one good and one bad battery.
* Test ride the scooters using the new minimum voltage of 34V.


How SOC is calculated currently by the IoT on the Välk scooters:

{code:c}u32 battery_calculate_percentage(void)
{
#define VOLTAGE_MAX 41.5
#define VOLTAGE_MIN 32.0
    Ql_OS_TakeMutex(adc_mutex);
    float measured_voltage = battery_info.scooter_battery_filtered_voltage;
    Ql_OS_GiveMutex(adc_mutex);
    u32 battery_percentage;
    if (measured_voltage > VOLTAGE_MAX)
    {
        battery_percentage = 100;
    }
    else if (measured_voltage >= VOLTAGE_MIN)
    {
        battery_percentage = 100 * (measured_voltage - VOLTAGE_MIN) / (VOLTAGE_MAX - VOLTAGE_MIN);
    }
    else // measured_voltage < VOLTAGE_MIN
    {
        battery_percentage = 0;
    }
    LOG_TRACE("Battery percentage: %u", battery_percentage);
    return battery_percentage;
#undef VOLTAGE_MAX
#undef VOLTAGE_MIN
}{code}

After speaking with PingCAP, this is what they think:

{quote}Hi [@leandro.morgado|https://bolt.enterprise.slack.com/team/U01AH7VKNHY], hope everything it's fine with you. I was able to do some research based on the telemetry provided and some internal discussion with our colleagues. The thing is that is very difficult to narrow down to a single execution since that there are constantly spikes in between and on both clinics and since executions below 300 ms don't appear in slow queries, but we see a few slow execution in both of the clinics(300 ms - 700 ms). Having this, we were able to see a pattern and understand in general what is going on. It seems that in the time of the slow execution point_get operator might be affected by excessive scans on MVCC keys(Total(next) > Processed_keys). What is going on is that in the moment of slow execution there too many old versions of same key and TIKV needs to go through all of them to find the correct version visible; this behavior leads to I\O pressure. Since that GC lifetime is 10 min by default, a solution will be to enable TIKV MVCC In-Memory Engine feature to accelerate query, since it needs to scan a large number of MVCC historical versions. Here you can find documentation on how to achieve that. [https://docs.pingcap.com/tidb/stable/tikv-in-memory-engine/#tikv-mvcc-in-memory-engine|https://docs.pingcap.com/tidb/stable/tikv-in-memory-engine/#tikv-mvcc-in-memory-engine|smart-link]   You need to update TIKV config file and restart it to take effect. The other parameters in the documentation can be modified dynamically.

{quote}



{quote}# Yes, it seems so based on the metrics that we have. There is a correlation between slow query execution and increased number of MVCC scans;
# This is showed by tikv_wall_time, which represents  the total time between entering the operator and leaving the operator, more or less how long TIKV spend from start to finish on the request. Although, i totally agree with you that more information should be visible on where it is spent and what could mean. Usually, high tikv_wall_time means spikes of unified read pool CPU or reading cold data/multiple versions of same key that has to wait for I\O;
# Based on the print-screen that i've shared previously from Grafana, if we have a big gap between total keys scan and processed keys(total>>processed), then this implies we are talking about excessive MVCC scans. [https://docs.pingcap.com/tidb/stable/tikv-in-memory-engine/#tikv-mvcc-in-memory-engine|https://docs.pingcap.com/tidb/stable/tikv-in-memory-engine/#tikv-mvcc-in-memory-engine|smart-link] {quote}

