Hello again everyone, and thank you for the additional information provided here and in Slack.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
Generally, co-promotion campaigns like this would fall under the definition of third-party offers, which often (but not always) require hard opt-in consent from B2B recipients to receive such direct marketing communications. You can find more detail in our [self-service guidelines|https://taxify.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/LEG/pages/5465899306/Marketing+Privacy+Guidelines] under the section *“Third-party offers.”*

However, I note that we do not have any functionality to collect consent from admins, which means that either consent must be collected ad hoc, OR a risk-based business decision must be made to proceed as is, without hard opt-in consent.
{panel}

*Risks:*

I can generally summarise that because:

* it's a B2B offer;
* the scope is limited to Bolt Business and is an ad hoc campaign;
* the offer is generally beneficial to Bolt Business customers;
* the offer would be limited to one channel (email);

the risks to proceed without hard opt-in consents in all of the countries *are low to medium*.

{panel:bgColor=#eae6ff}
The main risk is that the business admins may complain about receiving the offer to their local data protection authorities, which is unlikely considering the factors above.

However, considering the scope of affected countries, some of them are considered high risk in terms of enforcement probability, which means that IF complaints are received by local authorities, they are likely to be investigated, and Bolt may face a warning or a limited fine (most likely under 100K EUR).
{panel}

